WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.392
A good classification rule should make its boundaries easy to explain.

00:00:04.392 --> 00:00:09.317
In the billing example, we first handle NULL as Unknown.

00:00:09.317 --> 00:00:13.052
Then bills of ten thousand or more are High-Value.

00:00:13.052 --> 00:00:17.552
Bills of five thousand or more, but below ten thousand, are Standard.

00:00:17.552 --> 00:00:20.482
Remaining known values are Low-Value.

00:00:20.482 --> 00:00:27.967
Because we write the thresholds from high to low, we do not need to manually state an upper bound on the

00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:35.047
Standard branch. If the amount were ten thousand or more, it would already have matched High-Value.

00:00:35.047 --> 00:00:39.877
Boundary testing is essential. Ask what happens at exactly ten thousand.

00:00:39.877 --> 00:00:43.922
It should be High-Value. What happens at exactly five thousand?

00:00:43.922 --> 00:00:48.432
It should be Standard. What happens at four thousand nine hundred ninety-nine?

00:00:48.432 --> 00:00:53.117
Low-Value. Later, we add a No Charge category for exactly zero.

00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:57.242
That is a good example of domain knowledge changing the rule.

00:00:57.242 --> 00:01:04.162
Zero is technically less than five thousand, so without a special branch it would be labeled Low-Value.

00:01:04.162 --> 00:01:09.417
But in the business context, No Charge communicates a different meaning.

00:01:09.417 --> 00:01:16.327
Whenever you create tiers, write down the threshold values and test directly on those boundaries.

00:01:16.327 --> 00:01:21.788
Most classification bugs happen near edges, not in the obvious middle cases.
